{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.72,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["U2"],
      "pins": ["U2.1"],
      "nets": ["VCC", "VBAT"],
      "obs": "On the root sheet (PCB.kicad_sch), the top-edge power pins of U2 (STM32L476RGT6) are wired to the VCC rail: junction dots join pins 21 (VDD), 43 (VDD) and 49 (VDDA) to the VCC arrow. However, the stub from pin 1 (VBAT) is marked with a red no-connect flag (X) at the point it crosses the VCC line, leaving VBAT floating — no other wire, battery, or diode connects to it anywhere on the drawing.",
      "exp": "Standard STM32L4 practice (and the reference design) requires VBAT to be tied to VCC when no backup battery is present. The spec confirms no battery/regulator exists on the board ('no battery, regulator, or USB part appears anywhere'), so VBAT must be connected to VCC like the other VDD pins.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Root sheet, top of U2: '1 VBAT' stub carries a red no-connect X at the VCC rail, while pins 21 VDD, 43 VDD, 49 VDDA have junction dots on the same VCC wire"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "no battery, regulator, or USB part appears anywhere in the three .kicad_sch files or PCB.net/PCB.cir"}
      ],
      "impact": "The MCU's VBAT/backup-domain supply pin is left unconnected, so the RTC/backup domain and the internal power-path for VBAT are unpowered; depending on the device this can prevent proper start-up, cause the backup domain to be unpowered, or produce undefined behaviour versus the intended VBAT-to-VCC connection.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "All buses, pull-ups, decoupling, TSC, NFC antenna and OLED charge-pump connections match the specification, but U2 pin 1 (VBAT) carries a no-connect marker where it should join the VCC rail (no backup battery exists on this board), leaving the VBAT pin floating — a deviation from the intended design."
}

